🔹 What is Pattern Matching?
Pattern Matching makes it easier to check an object’s type and extract values in a single step — instead of writing boilerplate instanceof and casting code.

🔹 Old Way (Before Java 14)

if (obj instanceof String) {
    String s = (String) obj;  // manual cast
    System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
}

🔹 New Way (Pattern Matching for instanceof)

if (obj instanceof String s) {
    // 's' is already casted
    System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
}

✅ No explicit cast needed.
✅ Variable s is scoped only inside the if (flow scoping).

🔹 Flow scoping example

if (!(obj instanceof String s)) {
    return;
}
// yahan s use ho sakta hai — compiler jaanta hai type String hai
System.out.println(s.length());

🔹 Guarded patterns (when clause)

Guarded pattern = type match + extra condition (`when`).
Matlab: pehle type check, phir usi variable pe condition.

Syntax:
  case Type variable when condition -> ...

Example:

static void printLength(Object obj) {
    switch (obj) {
        case String s when s.length() > 5 -> System.out.println("Long text: " + s);
        case String s                     -> System.out.println("Short text: " + s);
        default                           -> System.out.println("Not a String");
    }
}

Dry run (samajhne ke liye):
  printLength("HelloWorld");  // String + length 10 > 5  → "Long: HelloWorld"
  printLength("Hi");          // String + length 2 ≤ 5   → "Short: Hi"
  printLength(42);            // not a String             → "Not a String"

Bina `when` ke aapko aisa likhna padta:

case String s -> {
    if (s.length() > 5) System.out.println("Long: " + s);
    else System.out.println("Short: " + s);
}

`when` us if ko case ke andar hi fold kar deta hai.

⚡ Dominance rule — case order matters
Cases top → bottom try hote hain. Specific (guarded) pehle, general baad mein.

✅ Correct order:

switch (obj) {
    case String s when s.length() > 5 -> System.out.println("Long");  // specific
    case String s                     -> System.out.println("Short"); // general
    default                           -> System.out.println("Other");
}

❌ Wrong order (compile error — unreachable case):

switch (obj) {
    case String s                     -> System.out.println("Short"); // sab Strings catch
    case String s when s.length() > 5 -> System.out.println("Long");  // kabhi nahi chalega
}

Reason: unguarded `case String s` already covers EVERY String,
so guarded case ke neeche rehna impossible → compiler reject.

Ek aur example (Integer):

static String checkNumber(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Integer i when i > 0  -> "Positive: " + i;
        case Integer i when i < 0  -> "Negative: " + i;
        case Integer i             -> "Zero";          // i == 0
        case String s              -> "Not a number: " + s;
        default                    -> "Unknown";
    };
}

// checkNumber(10)   → "Positive: 10"
// checkNumber(-3)   → "Negative: -3"
// checkNumber(0)    → "Zero"
// checkNumber("hi") → "Not a number: hi"



🔹 Pattern Matching + Sealed Classes + switch
Here’s where sealed classes shine:

sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Square {}

final class Circle implements Shape {
    double radius;
    Circle(double r) { this.radius = r; }
}

final class Rectangle implements Shape {
    double length, width;
    Rectangle(double l, double w) { this.length = l; this.width = w; }
}

final class Square implements Shape {
    double side;
    Square(double s) { this.side = s; }
}

Now use Pattern Matching in a switch:

static double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius * c.radius;
        case Rectangle r -> r.length * r.width;
        case Square s    -> s.side * s.side;
    };
}

⚡ Notice:
- No default is required because compiler knows all subtypes (Circle, Rectangle, Square) from the sealed hierarchy.
- Each case directly binds the type (Circle c) and variable (c).



🔹 Record + Pattern Matching (Super Powerful)
With records, pattern matching can destructure objects:

record Point(int x, int y) {}

static String printPoint(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Point(int x, int y) -> "Point at (" + x + ", " + y + ")";
        case String s            -> "String: " + s;
        case null                -> "Null value";
        default                  -> "Something else";
    };
}

✅ Nested record patterns bhi possible hain:

record Point(int x, int y) {}
record Rectangle(Point topLeft, Point bottomRight) {}

static int width(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Rectangle(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2)) -> x2 - x1;
        default -> 0;
    };
}



🧠 Interview one-liner
“Pattern Matching lets you test a type and bind a variable in one step — with instanceof (Java 16)
and switch (Java 21) — cutting cast boilerplate and enabling exhaustive matching with sealed types.”

✅ Summary
- Pattern Matching simplifies instanceof + casting.
- Works with switch expressions for sealed classes (exhaustive, no default needed).
- Combined with records, it enables destructuring.
- Guarded patterns (when) add conditions on cases.
- Removes boilerplate and makes code more concise + type-safe.
